/*
 * LC 325 — Maximum Size Subarray Sum Equals k
 * (LeetCode Premium — also on GeeksforGeeks / Coding Ninjas free)
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * Given array nums and integer k, find length of LONGEST subarray
 * that sums to k. Return 0 if none exists.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - Negatives possible → sliding window won't work, need prefix sum + HashMap
 * - Want longest length → store first index, never update
 *
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * THE THEORY — SIMPLE WORDS
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * We walk through keeping a running total.
 * For each position, we ask:
 *   "Have I seen a running total of (current total minus k) before?"
 * If yes — the chunk between that old position and now adds up to k.
 * We remember each running total and the FIRST position we saw it.
 * First only — because we want the longest possible chunk.
 * When we find a match, length = current index minus stored index.
 *
 * RULE OF THUMB:
 * found (currentSum - k) earlier → subarray between sums to k.
 *
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * WHY FIRST INDEX (never update)
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * We want LONGEST subarray → largest gap → earliest start.
 * So store first time a prefix sum appears, never overwrite.
 * (LC 560 counted subarrays so stored frequency and always updated —
 *  different goal, different storage.)
 *
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * BASE CASE {0 → -1}
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * Sum 0 exists before array starts.
 * Handles subarrays starting from index 0:
 *   if prefixSum == k → looking for 0 → found at -1
 *   → length = i - (-1) = i + 1 → whole prefix counted ✅
 *
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * COMPARISON — THE PREFIX SUM FAMILY
 * ════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * ┌──────────────┬───────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┐
 * │              │ LC 560        │ LC 525       │ LC 523       │ LC 325       │
 * ├──────────────┼───────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┤
 * │ Goal         │ count         │ longest len  │ exists?      │ longest len  │
 * │              │               │ (equal 0/1)  │ (multiple k) │ (sum = k)    │
 * │ Map key      │ prefixSum     │ prefixSum    │ prefixSum%k  │ prefixSum    │
 * │ Map value    │ frequency     │ first index  │ first index  │ first index  │
 * │ Map update   │ always        │ if absent    │ if absent    │ if absent    │
 * │ Look for     │ sum-k         │ same sum     │ same rem     │ sum-k        │
 * └──────────────┴───────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┘
 *
 * MASTER RULE:
 * - Counting subarrays    → store frequency, always update
 * - Longest/shortest      → store first index, never update
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬──────┬──────┐
 * │ Approach         │ Idea                             │ TC   │ SC   │
 * ├──────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼──────┼──────┤
 * │ Brute Force      │ Every subarray, track longest    │O(n²) │ O(1) │
 * │ Prefix+HashMap   │ prefixSum→first index            │ O(n) │ O(n) │
 * └──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴──────┴──────┘
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — BRUTE FORCE
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Simplest — check every subarray sum, track longest hitting k.
 * TC: O(n²) — all subarrays
 * SC: O(1)
 * REJECTED: too slow for large n
 */
public int maxSubArrayLenBrute(int[] nums, int k) {
    int maxLen = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int j = i; j < nums.length; j++) {
            sum += nums[j];
            if (sum == k) maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, j - i + 1);
        }
    }
    return maxLen;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 2 — PREFIX SUM + HASHMAP (Optimal)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * HOW:
 * map stores prefixSum → first index.
 * Initialize {0 → -1}.
 * For each i:
 *   prefixSum += nums[i]
 *   if (prefixSum - k) in map → maxLen = max(maxLen, i - map.get(prefixSum-k))
 *   if prefixSum NOT in map → store prefixSum → i (first occurrence only)
 *
 * Dry run — nums=[1,-1,5,-2,3], k=3:
 * map={0→-1}
 * i=0: sum=1, look -2 → no → store {0→-1,1→0}
 * i=1: sum=0, look -3 → no → 0 exists, don't update
 * i=2: sum=5, look 2  → no → store {0→-1,1→0,5→2}
 * i=3: sum=3, look 0  → YES at -1 → len=3-(-1)=4, maxLen=4
 * i=4: sum=6, look 3  → no → store 6→4
 * return 4 ✅
 *
 * TC: O(n) — single pass
 * SC: O(n) — map stores distinct prefix sums
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - No subarray sums to k → maxLen stays 0 ✅
 * - Negatives → handled, prefix sums can repeat/decrease ✅
 * - Whole array sums to k → base case {0→-1} gives full length ✅
 */
public int maxSubArrayLen(int[] nums, int k) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
    map.put(0, -1); // base case — sum 0 before array starts
    int prefixSum = 0, maxLen = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        prefixSum += nums[i];
        // a previous prefix sum makes current chunk = k
        if (map.containsKey(prefixSum - k)) {
            maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, i - map.get(prefixSum - k));
        }
        // store first occurrence only — want longest, so keep earliest index
        if (!map.containsKey(prefixSum)) {
            map.put(prefixSum, i);
        }
    }
    return maxLen;
}
